Scott states the domain equations \(B\cong B+B\) and \(C\cong\{\{\Lambda\}\}+C+C\), and over \(\{0,1\}^*\) the neighbourhood equations \(B=\{\{0,1\}^*\}\cup\{0X\mid X\in B\}\cup\{1X\mid X\in B\}\) and \(C=\{\{0,1\}^*\}\cup\{\{\Lambda\}\}\cup\{0X\mid X\in C\}\cup\{1X\mid X\in C\}\); he solves \(A\cong A^n+A^n\) by \(A=\{\{0,1\}^*\}\cup\bigcup_{i\in\{0,1\}}\{i\bigcup_{j<n}1^j0X_j\mid X_j\in A\}\), so that for \(n=3\) a typical neighbourhood is \(00X_0\cup 010X_1\cup 0110X_2\) with the leading bit free to be \(1\), reads a non-bottom element as \(\pm\langle a_0,\ldots,a_{n-1}\rangle\) with \(+\) on the right and \(-\) on the left, and treats a total element as an infinite \(n\)-ary \(\pm\)-tree, partial where \(\bot\) appears and finite when every branch ends at \(\bot\). Selectors satisfy \(a\Lambda=a\) and \(ai\sigma=(a_i)\sigma\), \(\mathrm{pos}\) sends a \(+\) root to true and a \(-\) root to false, a total tree is eventually periodic when \(\{a\sigma\}\) is finite, and \(L_a=\{\sigma\mid\mathrm{pos}(a\sigma)=\mathrm{true}\}\) is then a regular event, every regular language arising in this way, with \(a\) the start state and \(a\sigma\) the state after reading \(\sigma\). The same note then requires a category of systems and approximable maps, records that forbidding the empty neighbourhood loses no generality up to isomorphism and that strict maps form another category, and defines the functor \(T(X)=D+(X\times X)\) by \(T(X)=\{\Gamma'\}\cup\{0X\mid X\in D\}\cup\{10X\cup 11Y\mid X,Y\in X\}\) with \(\Gamma'=0\Delta\cup 10\Gamma\cup 11\Gamma\). `memB_cases` is the equation for \(B\): a neighbourhood is the cone of a finite binary string, the empty prefix is the master of all strings, and a string headed by false or true is `embBit` of the cone of its tail, where `embBit` prepends that bit (`embBit_eq_prepend`, `embBit_cone`), false standing for \(0\) and true for \(1\). `toBB` puts the left injection of \(X\) into the image exactly when the element contains the false tagging, and the right injection of \(Y\) exactly when it contains the true tagging; `fromBB` reverses the clauses; `fromBB_toBB` and `toBB_fromBB` are the round trips on the three shapes, using `embBit_ne_univ`, `embBit_injective`, and `embBit_inter_ne` to exclude the master and the opposite bit; `bbEquiv` is the order isomorphism and `B_domain_equation` is \(B\cong B+B\). `memC_cases` is the equation for \(C\), with \(\{\{\Lambda\}\}\) the singleton of the empty word (`singleton_nil_ne_univ`, `singleton_nil_ne_embBit`); `toCC` and `fromCC` match that singleton with the unit injection `j0` and the false and true embeddings with `j1` and `j2`; `ccEquiv` and `C_domain_equation` are \(C\cong\mathbf{1}+C+C\). For positive \(n\), `slotPre` is the bit \(i\), then \(j\) ones, then a zero, and `embTuple` is the union of those slots, so the false \(n=3\) case is \(00X_0\cup 010X_1\cup 0110X_2\); `MemA` is the least family containing the set of all strings and every tagged tuple whose components are already in the family, `Asys` is that neighbourhood system, and `prodN`, `npow`, and `Apow` are the \(n\)-fold power. `toAA` and `fromAA` send false tuples to the left injection and true tuples to the right, with false as \(-\) and true as \(+\); `aaEquiv` is the order isomorphism and `A_domain_equation` is \(A\cong A^n+A^n\), including the one-step reading of a non-bottom element as a left or right \(n\)-tuple. The round trips `fromAA_toAA` and `toAA_fromAA` case on the universe and the two tags, and the cross cases die by `embTuple_ne_univ`, `embTuple_injective`, and `embTuple_inter_ne`, using a nonempty component at index \(0\) (`memA_nonempty`). On the tree side, `Tree` is a total labeling of words on \(\mathrm{Fin}\,n\) by a Boolean, with true as \(+\), and is not an element of `Asys`; `select_nil` is \(a\Lambda=a\), `select_cons` is \(ai\sigma=(a_i)\sigma\), `pos` is the root label, and `treeLang_eq_pos` is \(L_a=\{\sigma\mid\mathrm{pos}(a\sigma)=\mathrm{true}\}\). `EventuallyPeriodic` is finiteness of the set of subtrees. `treeLang_select` shows that the language of the subtree at \(\sigma\) is the left quotient by \(\sigma\), `treeLang_injective` shows that the language determines the tree, and `eventuallyPeriodic_iff_isRegular` rewrites regularity as finiteness of the set of left quotients and carries finiteness across that injection; `isRegular_iff_exists_eventuallyPeriodic` realizes any regular language by its characteristic tree, classically. That quotient argument is Scott’s automaton, states being the subtrees. The `Basic` gloss of this development says the subtree itself is the left quotient, which is not what `treeLang_select` proves and not what Scott says. No stem unfolds a total element of \(|A|\) to a `Tree`, defines \(\mathrm{pos}\) on \(|A|\), or treats partial or finite elements, and no imported stem builds the category, the empty-neighbourhood deletion, strict maps, or \(T(X)=D+(X\times X)\) with the encoding of \(\Gamma'\). **Theorem:** partial — \(B\cong B+B\), \(C\cong\{\{\Lambda\}\}+C+C\), and \(A\cong A^n+A^n\) for positive \(n\), with the string equations and the regularity of eventually periodic total \(\pm\)-trees, are proved; the trees are not identified with total elements of \(A\), and \(T\) is not defined. **Proof:** partial — the isomorphisms are the round-trip case splits above and regularity is the Myhill–Nerode argument, while the representation of elements of \(A\) and the construction of \(T\) are absent.
